{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C7MBSyVfLwqzR9K2kPLKBD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ABmwNtxoSvH6DMETU8kjFH","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer R1** for a robotics/ML conference (CoRL). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue Standards (CoRL)\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=seminal, 8-9=strong accept, 7=accept, 6=marginal accept, 5=marginal reject, 4=reject, 3=clear reject, 1-2=strong reject)\n- Acceptance rate: ~40%\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality (technical soundness), Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning, Limitations\n- Papers must demonstrate relevance to robot learning through intent or outcome\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: Decomposing Collision Avoidance into Implicit Demonstrations and Explicit Geometric Supervision\"\n\nThe paper studies collision avoidance in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models on SafeLIBERO. Key claims:\n\n1. **Fake Safety (Claim 1):** Pi0 model with SDF loss achieves 91.5% CAR but TSR=0% — action collapse, not real avoidance. They propose displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR) that filters out frozen episodes. dfCAR is robust across δ∈[2mm,100mm].\n\n2. **Decomposing Avoidance (Claim 2-3):** LoRA fine-tuning on collision-free demos produces +29pp CAR over baseline (implicit demo signals). SDF auxiliary loss at low λ (≤0.01) adds no significant CAR above LoRA-only. At λ=0.1, SDF adds +15.5pp CAR (p<0.02) — dose-dependent effect.\n\n3. **Two-Phase Training (Claim 4):** SDF pre-training (10K steps) → task-only fine-tuning → CAR=66%, TSR=40% (2-seed mean), partially resolving safety-capability tradeoff.\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n\n**Models:** Pi0 (condition A1), Pi0.5 baseline, LoRA fine-tuned Pi0.5. OpenVLA planned but BLOCKED (model download issues — only projected values reported).\n\n**Evaluation:** 200 episodes per condition (50 per task), object_pudding scene (4 tasks). Metrics: TSR, CAR, EE displacement, frozen rate.\n\n**Tables:**\n- Table 1: Fake safety diagnosis. A1: TSR=0%, CAR=91%, Fake Safe Rate=99.4%, EE Disp (safe eps)=0.005mm. Pi0.5 baseline: TSR=73%, CAR=28%. OpenVLA projected: TSR=0%, CAR=88%.\n- Table 2: dfCAR threshold sensitivity. A1 dfCAR=0.4% at all thresholds. LoRA dfCAR=54.5% constant. SDF λ=0.005 dfCAR=52.3% constant. Baseline dfCAR=83→80% (mild decrease).\n- Table 3: LoRA-only 3 seeds. s0: TSR=8.5%, CAR=54.5%. s1: TSR=4%, CAR=56%. s2: TSR=2%, CAR=44%. Mean CAR=51.5%, SD=6.5pp.\n- Table 4: 3v3 SDF attribution. LoRA-only CAR=51.5% (SE=3.7pp). SDF λ=0.005: 52.3% (SE=1.5pp, Δ=+0.8pp, p>0.5). SDF λ=0.01: 52.8% (SE=0.3pp, Δ=+1.3pp, p>0.3). SDF λ=0.1: 67% (SE=1.0pp, Δ=+15.5pp, p<0.02).\n- Table 5: Lambda dose-response. Flat for λ≤0.01, jumps at λ=0.1.\n- Table 6: Two-phase results. Phase 2 seed 2: CAR=68%, TSR=42%. Phase 2 seed 0: CAR=64%, TSR=38%. Mean: CAR=66%, TSR=40%.\n- Table 7: Cross-scene. object_pudding: LoRA CAR=51.5%, SDF CAR=52.3%, Δ=+0.8pp (ns). spatial_bowl: LoRA CAR=36%, SDF CAR=45%, Δ=+9pp (marginal). NOT consistent across scenes.\n- Table 8: Training convergence. All stable.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 15 experiments. Key gaps relative to the paper:\n- **plan_002 (OpenVLA zero-shot):** BLOCKED. Paper reports only projected values. Only 2 model families tested.\n- **plan_012 (Phase 2 additional seed):** Only 2 seeds for two-phase → low statistical power (SE≈±2pp).\n- **plan_013 (LoRA seed2):** CAR=44%, driving high LoRA variance (SD=6.5pp). Makes low-λ SDF attribution unreliable.\n- **plan_015 (spatial_bowl LoRA control):** Only 1 seed per condition. Cross-scene SDF effect inconsistent.\n- Phase 2 only tested from λ=0.005 10K checkpoint, not explored other Phase 1 durations or λ values.\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental Design Quality:**\n   - Are the ablations well-controlled? Is the 3v3 comparison properly powered?\n   - Is 200 episodes per condition sufficient? Is 50 per task enough?\n   - How does the high LoRA variance (SD=6.5pp) affect the statistical claims?\n   - Is the p<0.02 for λ=0.1 reliable given only 3 seeds per condition?\n\n2. **Statistical Rigor:**\n   - Are the statistical tests appropriate? What test was used for the p-values?\n   - With only 3 seeds, what is the effective degrees of freedom?\n   - The LoRA SE=3.7pp vs SDF SE=1.0pp — is unequal variance handled?\n   - Two-phase has only 2 seeds (SE≈±2pp) — can this support a claim?\n\n3. **Methodological Concerns:**\n   - dfCAR definition: Is excluding episodes with low displacement principled? Does it introduce bias?\n   - SDF loss formulation (Eq. 2): EE-only, cannot prevent arm-body collisions. How limiting is this?\n   - Two-phase protocol: Only one Phase 1 duration (10K) and one λ (0.005) tested. How robust?\n   - The \"fake safety\" phenomenon — is this genuinely novel or well-known (action collapse)?\n\n4. **Missing Experiments / Gaps:**\n   - OpenVLA blocked — weakens generalization claim\n   - No real robot experiments\n   - Single scene (object_pudding) for main results; cross-scene inconsistent\n   - No comparison with other safety methods (e.g., constrained RL, safety filters)\n   - Phase 2 not explored with different Phase 1 durations or λ values\n\n5. **Reproducibility:**\n   - Are training details sufficient? (LoRA rank, learning rate, etc. not specified in paper)\n   - Is the code/benchmark available?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's method and experimental contributions]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 4-8 items, each with specific evidence]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What additional experiments would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, presentation issues, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: [1-4] (1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Clarity: [1-4]\n- Overall: [1-10] with justification\n- Confidence: [1-5] (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":608,"cache_read_input_tokens":63471,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":608},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c2499d0-d3bb-4c09-8644-35391fa8435e","uuid":"01b98d27-6f86-43ba-9ffc-a6aae2f5e53d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aab3c4458efb3fb45","tool_use_id":"toolu_01ABmwNtxoSvH6DMETU8kjFH","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are **Reviewer R1** for a robotics/ML conference (CoRL). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue Standards (CoRL)\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=seminal, 8-9=strong accept, 7=accept, 6=marginal accept, 5=marginal reject, 4=reject, 3=clear reject, 1-2=strong reject)\n- Acceptance rate: ~40%\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality (technical soundness), Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning, Limitations\n- Papers must demonstrate relevance to robot learning through intent or outcome\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: Decomposing Collision Avoidance into Implicit Demonstrations and Explicit Geometric Supervision\"\n\nThe paper studies collision avoidance in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models on SafeLIBERO. Key claims:\n\n1. **Fake Safety (Claim 1):** Pi0 model with SDF loss achieves 91.5% CAR but TSR=0% — action collapse, not real avoidance. They propose displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR) that filters out frozen episodes. dfCAR is robust across δ∈[2mm,100mm].\n\n2. **Decomposing Avoidance (Claim 2-3):** LoRA fine-tuning on collision-free demos produces +29pp CAR over baseline (implicit demo signals). SDF auxiliary loss at low λ (≤0.01) adds no significant CAR above LoRA-only. At λ=0.1, SDF adds +15.5pp CAR (p<0.02) — dose-dependent effect.\n\n3. **Two-Phase Training (Claim 4):** SDF pre-training (10K steps) → task-only fine-tuning → CAR=66%, TSR=40% (2-seed mean), partially resolving safety-capability tradeoff.\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n\n**Models:** Pi0 (condition A1), Pi0.5 baseline, LoRA fine-tuned Pi0.5. OpenVLA planned but BLOCKED (model download issues — only projected values reported).\n\n**Evaluation:** 200 episodes per condition (50 per task), object_pudding scene (4 tasks). Metrics: TSR, CAR, EE displacement, frozen rate.\n\n**Tables:**\n- Table 1: Fake safety diagnosis. A1: TSR=0%, CAR=91%, Fake Safe Rate=99.4%, EE Disp (safe eps)=0.005mm. Pi0.5 baseline: TSR=73%, CAR=28%. OpenVLA projected: TSR=0%, CAR=88%.\n- Table 2: dfCAR threshold sensitivity. A1 dfCAR=0.4% at all thresholds. LoRA dfCAR=54.5% constant. SDF λ=0.005 dfCAR=52.3% constant. Baseline dfCAR=83→80% (mild decrease).\n- Table 3: LoRA-only 3 seeds. s0: TSR=8.5%, CAR=54.5%. s1: TSR=4%, CAR=56%. s2: TSR=2%, CAR=44%. Mean CAR=51.5%, SD=6.5pp.\n- Table 4: 3v3 SDF attribution. LoRA-only CAR=51.5% (SE=3.7pp). SDF λ=0.005: 52.3% (SE=1.5pp, Δ=+0.8pp, p>0.5). SDF λ=0.01: 52.8% (SE=0.3pp, Δ=+1.3pp, p>0.3). SDF λ=0.1: 67% (SE=1.0pp, Δ=+15.5pp, p<0.02).\n- Table 5: Lambda dose-response. Flat for λ≤0.01, jumps at λ=0.1.\n- Table 6: Two-phase results. Phase 2 seed 2: CAR=68%, TSR=42%. Phase 2 seed 0: CAR=64%, TSR=38%. Mean: CAR=66%, TSR=40%.\n- Table 7: Cross-scene. object_pudding: LoRA CAR=51.5%, SDF CAR=52.3%, Δ=+0.8pp (ns). spatial_bowl: LoRA CAR=36%, SDF CAR=45%, Δ=+9pp (marginal). NOT consistent across scenes.\n- Table 8: Training convergence. All stable.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 15 experiments. Key gaps relative to the paper:\n- **plan_002 (OpenVLA zero-shot):** BLOCKED. Paper reports only projected values. Only 2 model families tested.\n- **plan_012 (Phase 2 additional seed):** Only 2 seeds for two-phase → low statistical power (SE≈±2pp).\n- **plan_013 (LoRA seed2):** CAR=44%, driving high LoRA variance (SD=6.5pp). Makes low-λ SDF attribution unreliable.\n- **plan_015 (spatial_bowl LoRA control):** Only 1 seed per condition. Cross-scene SDF effect inconsistent.\n- Phase 2 only tested from λ=0.005 10K checkpoint, not explored other Phase 1 durations or λ values.\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental Design Quality:**\n   - Are the ablations well-controlled? Is the 3v3 comparison properly powered?\n   - Is 200 episodes per condition sufficient? Is 50 per task enough?\n   - How does the high LoRA variance (SD=6.5pp) affect the statistical claims?\n   - Is the p<0.02 for λ=0.1 reliable given only 3 seeds per condition?\n\n2. **Statistical Rigor:**\n   - Are the statistical tests appropriate? What test was used for the p-values?\n   - With only 3 seeds, what is the effective degrees of freedom?\n   - The LoRA SE=3.7pp vs SDF SE=1.0pp — is unequal variance handled?\n   - Two-phase has only 2 seeds (SE≈±2pp) — can this support a claim?\n\n3. **Methodological Concerns:**\n   - dfCAR definition: Is excluding episodes with low displacement principled? Does it introduce bias?\n   - SDF loss formulation (Eq. 2): EE-only, cannot prevent arm-body collisions. How limiting is this?\n   - Two-phase protocol: Only one Phase 1 duration (10K) and one λ (0.005) tested. How robust?\n   - The \"fake safety\" phenomenon — is this genuinely novel or well-known (action collapse)?\n\n4. **Missing Experiments / Gaps:**\n   - OpenVLA blocked — weakens generalization claim\n   - No real robot experiments\n   - Single scene (object_pudding) for main results; cross-scene inconsistent\n   - No comparison with other safety methods (e.g., constrained RL, safety filters)\n   - Phase 2 not explored with different Phase 1 durations or λ values\n\n5. **Reproducibility:**\n   - Are training details sufficient? (LoRA rank, learning rate, etc. not specified in paper)\n   - Is the code/benchmark available?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's method and experimental contributions]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 4-8 items, each with specific evidence]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What additional experiments would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, presentation issues, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: [1-4] (1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Clarity: [1-4]\n- Overall: [1-10] with justification\n- Confidence: [1-5] (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n","uuid":"6cfc225c-a4ec-482c-963a-a1b8bc297d84","session_id":"9c2499d0-d3bb-4c09-8644-35391fa8435e"}
