k\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 4-8 items, each with specific evidence]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be cited]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Formatting, writing, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: [1-4] (1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Clarity: [1-4]\n- Overall: [1-10] with justification\n- Confidence: [1-5] (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n","uuid":"c78d2b1f-c2b0-4799-aa24-28a3dafd3b22","session_id":"9c2499d0-d3bb-4c09-8644-35391fa8435e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are **Reviewer R2** for a robotics/ML conference (CoRL). Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue Standards (CoRL)\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=seminal, 8-9=strong accept, 7=accept, 6=marginal accept, 5=marginal reject, 4=reject, 3=clear reject, 1-2=strong reject)\n- Acceptance rate: ~40%\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality (technical soundness), Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning, Limitations\n- Papers must demonstrate relevance to robot learning through intent or outcome\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: Decomposing Collision Avoidance into Implicit Demonstrations and Explicit Geometric Supervision\"\n\nThe paper studies collision avoidance in VLA models on SafeLIBERO benchmark. Three claims:\n\n1. **Fake Safety:** Pi0 with SDF loss achieves 91.5% CAR but TSR=0% — action collapse, not avoidance. Propose dfCAR metric that filters out frozen episodes. Robust across δ∈[2mm,100mm].\n\n2. **Decomposing Avoidance Sources:** LoRA fine-tuning on collision-free demos = dominant avoidance signal (+29pp CAR). SDF auxiliary loss only adds at high λ=0.1 (+15.5pp, p<0.02), not at low λ.\n\n3. **Two-Phase Training:** SDF pre-training → task fine-tuning achieves CAR=66%, TSR=40% (2-seed mean).\n\n## Paper Structure (7 pages)\n- **Abstract:** Claims fake safety identification, decomposition framework, two-phase curriculum\n- **Section 1 (Intro):** Motivates the problem, presents 3 findings\n- **Section 2 (Related Work):** 4 short paragraphs covering VLA Safety Evaluation, SDFs in Robotics, Safety-Capability Tradeoffs, Curriculum/Multi-Phase Training. All citations are \"[?]\" placeholders.\n- **Section 3 (Method):** Problem setup, dfCAR definition (Eq. 1), SDF loss (Eq. 2), Two-phase protocol\n- **Section 4 (Experiments):** 8 subsections with 8 tables\n- **Section 5 (Discussion):** Decomposition vs Redundancy, High LoRA Variance, Two-Phase Promise, Scene-Dependent Effects, Limitations (5 items)\n- **Section 6 (Conclusion):** Summary of 3 findings + guidance\n\n## Detailed Content for Related Work Assessment\n\n**Section 2 full text:**\n- VLA Safety Evaluation: SafeLIBERO [?] introduced CAR. Paper claims to identify systematic contamination by action collapse and propose dfCAR.\n- SDFs in Robotics: SDFs in motion planning [??]. Vision-learned SDFs for avoidance [?]. Paper uses SDF as training-time signal, not runtime.\n- Safety-Capability Tradeoffs: Safe RL literature [?]. Paper's finding echoes conservatism-capability tradeoff but from different mechanism (demo learning vs explicit safety objective).\n- Curriculum/Multi-Phase Training: Progressive training [?]. Paper's two-phase = novel application of curriculum learning to VLA safety.\n\n**CRITICAL: ALL CITATIONS ARE PLACEHOLDERS \"[?]\"** — no actual references in the paper.\n\n## Key Experimental Numbers for Novelty Assessment\n- Fake safety: A1 CAR=91%, TSR=0%, EE disp=0.005mm in safe eps\n- dfCAR: threshold-robust across 3 orders of magnitude\n- LoRA implicit signal: +29pp CAR over baseline\n- SDF dose-response: flat at λ≤0.01, jumps +15.5pp at λ=0.1\n- Two-phase: CAR=66%, TSR=40% vs LoRA-only TSR=5%\n- Cross-scene inconsistency: SDF redundant in object_pudding, additive in spatial_bowl\n\n## Experiment Coverage Gaps (from review_context.yaml)\n- OpenVLA experiment BLOCKED → only 2 model families\n- Only 2 seeds for two-phase\n- spatial_bowl: 1 seed per condition\n- LoRA seed2 low CAR (44%) drives high variance (SD=6.5pp)\n- No exploration of different Phase 1 durations or λ values\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Novelty and Related Work**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment:**\n   - Is \"fake safety\" / action collapse genuinely novel? Action collapse is well-known in RL and robot learning.\n   - Is dfCAR a significant metric contribution? It's essentially filtering by displacement — how different from simply reporting TSR alongside CAR?\n   - Is the decomposition into implicit (demo) vs explicit (SDF) signals a novel insight? Or obvious in retrospect?\n   - Is the dose-response finding (threshold effect at λ=0.1) novel or expected?\n   - Is the two-phase curriculum genuinely novel for VLA safety?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness:**\n   - ALL citations are \"[?]\" placeholders — this is a major formatting/completeness issue\n   - Missing related work areas:\n     * Safe reinforcement learning (constrained MDPs, Lagrangian methods, etc.)\n     * Other VLA safety benchmarks beyond SafeLIBERO\n     * Action collapse / mode collapse in generative models / VLAs\n     * Reward shaping / auxiliary losses in RL\n     * Other geometric representations for safety (not just SDF)\n     * Sim-to-real transfer for safety evaluation\n   - Is the positioning relative to safe RL adequate?\n   - Is the curriculum learning framing appropriately contextualized?\n\n3. **Contribution Significance:**\n   - For CoRL (~40% acceptance): is this sufficient contribution?\n   - Is the paper primarily empirical observation or methodological contribution?\n   - The main \"method\" contributions (dfCAR, SDF loss, two-phase) are relatively simple — is the insight enough?\n   - Is the scope (single benchmark, 2 model families, 1 main scene) sufficient for the claims?\n\n4. **Framing and Positioning:**\n   - The title claims \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation\" — does the paper deliver?\n   - Is the \"decomposition framework\" really a framework or just an empirical observation?\n   - The paper acknowledges many limitations honestly — does this help or hurt?\n\n5. **Writing Quality:**\n   - Is the paper well-organized?\n   - Are the claims appropriately hedged?\n   - Is the discussion balanced?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 4-8 items, each with specific evidence]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be cited]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Formatting, writing, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: [1-4] (1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Clarity: [1-4]\n- Overall: [1-10] with justification\n- Confidence: [1-5] (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzJgRpakVmGTQ26MbPjsQK","session_id":"9c2499d0-d3bb-4c09-8644-35391fa8435e","uuid":"c2a42111-8d4c-4eca-9ad3-d28d7e62090f","timestamp":"2026-05-26T03:36:40.057Z"}
