{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ABmwNtxoSvH6DMETU8kjFH","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the context needed. Let me compose the detailed R1 review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThe paper diagnoses systematic contamination of the Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) metric on SafeLIBERO by action collapse (\"fake safety\"), proposes displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR), and conducts a series of ablations decomposing collision avoidance into implicit demonstration signals vs. explicit SDF geometric supervision. The experimental contribution spans 70+ experiments and 6000+ episodes across Pi0 and Pi0.5 model families. The central experimental finding is that LoRA fine-tuning on collision-free demonstrations alone accounts for the full +29pp CAR improvement, with SDF auxiliary loss contributing 0 additional pp at low lambda and a statistically significant +15.5pp only at lambda=0.1 (p<0.02), though the two-phase training protocol partially resolves the safety-capability tradeoff (CAR=66%, TSR=40%).\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-designed fake safety diagnosis (Claim 1).** The 800-episode A1 evaluation with per-episode displacement analysis provides compelling quantitative evidence. The dual evidence chain (99.6% obstacle displacement < 0.1mm AND TSR=0%) is methodologically sound. The bimodal displacement gap spanning 6 orders of magnitude (sub-micrometer vs. 200+ mm) makes the finding robust to analytic choices.\n\n- **Oracle experiment design is methodologically clean.** Testing three GT-SDF oracle variants (adaptive-alpha, fixed-alpha, feature-space) that all underperform the baseline (73-77% vs. 82% CAR) eliminates prediction-error confounds and cleanly isolates the EE-only structural limitation. This is a proper upper-bound experiment.\n\n- **dfCAR threshold sensitivity analysis is thorough.** Demonstrating invariance across delta in [2mm, 100mm] — a 50x range — is a strong robustness property. The fact that A1 dfCAR collapses to 0.4% at every threshold while LoRA dfCAR remains at 54.5% cleanly separates fake from real safety.\n\n- **The 3v3 seed design for SDF attribution (Table 4) is the correct experimental structure.** Using matched seed counts with separate standard errors and reporting p-values for each lambda condition follows good practice for isolating the SDF contribution from LoRA capacity effects.\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative results and iterative course corrections.** The decision log (D009-D010 terminating runtime refinement, D025 overturning SDF attribution) demonstrates scientific integrity. The paper does not hide inconvenient findings.\n\n- **Large cumulative experimental effort.** 70+ experiments, multiple model families, systematic lambda sweeps, phase-scheduled training — the experimental coverage is ambitious for a diagnostic paper.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The 3v3 comparison is critically underpowered for a null-result claim (Table 4).** With n=3 seeds per condition, the effective degrees of freedom for a Welch t-test is approximately 2-4. The LoRA-only condition has SD=6.5pp (SE=3.7pp), meaning the 95% CI on the LoRA-SDF difference at lambda=0.005 spans roughly [-8pp, +10pp]. The paper cannot distinguish \"SDF adds 0pp\" from \"SDF adds 5pp\" — or even \"SDF subtracts 5pp.\" The absence of a formal equivalence test (TOST) with a pre-specified margin is a significant methodological gap. Stating \"SDF contributes 0pp\" based on p>0.5 commits the classic error of treating absence-of-significance as evidence-of-absence. The LoRA seed2 anomaly (CAR=44% vs. s0=54.5%, s1=56%) is a 10pp outlier that drives the high variance and could flip the conclusion if replaced.\n\n- **Unequal variance between conditions is not addressed (Table 4).** LoRA-only SE=3.7pp vs. SDF lambda=0.005 SE=1.5pp represents a ~6x variance ratio. Standard Welch t-tests handle unequal variance but with n=3, the Satterthwaite approximation for degrees of freedom is unreliable. The paper does not report which statistical test was used, the degrees of freedom, or confidence intervals — only p-values. This is below the standard expected for a paper whose central claim rests on a null result.\n\n- **The lambda=0.1 result (Table 4, +15.5pp, p<0.02) is based on only 2 seeds.** A Welch t-test with 2 observations in one group and 3 in the other has at most 2-3 effective degrees of freedom. The p<0.02 from such a test has extremely fragile coverage properties — a single additional seed could easily push p above 0.05. Moreover, the jump from \"no effect at lambda<=0.01\" to \"+15.5pp at lambda=0.1\" (Table 5) with no intermediate lambda values (e.g., 0.03, 0.05) leaves the dose-response curve severely under-sampled. The paper cannot determine whether there is a smooth transition or a phase change, nor at what lambda the effect becomes meaningful.\n\n- **Two-phase training (Table 6) uses only 2 seeds.** The reported CAR=66% (SE~2pp) and TSR=40% (SE~2pp) cannot support strong claims. The 95% CI on TSR spans approximately [36%, 44%], which includes both \"practically useful\" and \"borderline useless\" territory. Only one Phase 1 duration (10K steps) and one lambda (0.005) were tested — the paper cannot determine whether these are optimal or merely the first configuration attempted. No ablation over Phase 1 duration (5K, 10K, 20K) or Phase 1 lambda (0.01, 0.05, 0.1) is provided.\n\n- **Cross-scene results (Table 7) are inconsistent and single-seeded.** The SDF effect on spatial_bowl (+9pp, marginal) differs qualitatively from object_pudding (+0.8pp, ns). With n=1 seed per condition for spatial_bowl, this discrepancy cannot be interpreted — it could reflect genuine scene-dependent effects or seed-level noise. The missing spatial_bowl LoRA-only control (plan_015) prevents proper attribution. The paper's claim that SDF is redundant is based primarily on one scene.\n\n- **The dfCAR metric, while sensible, introduces a potential selection bias.** By excluding low-displacement episodes, dfCAR conditions on a post-treatment variable (displacement). If the treatment (SDF loss) systematically changes which episodes have low displacement, the filtering can create spurious differences or mask real ones. The paper does not discuss this conditioning-on-a-mediator issue. Additionally, dfCAR is defined using EE displacement, but the A1 fake safety analysis (Table 1) uses obstacle displacement — these are different quantities and the paper does not reconcile them.\n\n- **The SDF loss formulation (Eq. 2 in the method) is EE-only, and the paper itself demonstrates this is structurally deficient (Claim 3, oracle experiments).** Testing SDF redundancy using a known-deficient SDF formulation creates circularity: the paper cannot distinguish \"geometric supervision is fundamentally redundant\" (strong, novel) from \"this particular EE-only formulation is too limited to help\" (weak, expected). The positive lambda=0.1 result actually undermines the redundancy claim — it suggests the SDF signal does work when strong enough, contradicting the \"redundant\" narrative.\n\n- **OpenVLA is blocked.** Table 1 reports only \"projected\" values for OpenVLA (TSR=0%, CAR=88%), but the actual evaluation was never completed due to model download failures. This weakens the generalization claim significantly — the paper tests only two model families (Pi0, Pi0.5) from the same architectural lineage. For an evaluation methodology paper claiming to identify a general problem with \"VLA Safety Evaluation,\" this is a critical scope limitation.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Statistical methodology:** What specific test was used for the p-values in Tables 4-5? Please report the test statistic, degrees of freedom, and 95% confidence intervals for each comparison. Have you considered a formal equivalence test (TOST) with a pre-registered equivalence margin? With n=3 seeds, what is the minimum detectable effect size at 80% power?\n\n2. **Lambda dose-response discontinuity:** The jump from \"no effect\" at lambda=0.01 to \"+15.5pp\" at lambda=0.1 is a 10x lambda increase with no intermediate data points. Have you tested lambda in {0.03, 0.05, 0.07}? Is this a smooth transition or a phase change? The lambda=0.1 result potentially contradicts the SDF-redundancy claim — how do you reconcile \"SDF adds nothing\" with \"10x more SDF adds 15.5pp\"?\n\n3. **Circularity in SDF redundancy claim:** Your oracle experiments (Claim 3) demonstrate that EE-only SDF is structurally deficient. You then test SDF redundancy using only EE-only SDF. How do you distinguish \"geometric supervision is fundamentally redundant for VLA collision avoidance\" from \"this particular EE-only SDF formulation is too broken to help at low doses\"?\n\n4. **TSR collapse mechanism:** Both LoRA-only and LoRA+SDF suffer identical catastrophic TSR collapse (73% to 6%). Is this catastrophic forgetting from fine-tuning on a filtered subset of demonstrations? Have you tried standard mitigations (EWC, replay buffer, lower learning rate, higher LoRA rank)? This is arguably the most practically consequential finding and deserves mechanistic analysis.\n\n5. **dfCAR selection bias:** Excluding low-displacement episodes conditions on a post-treatment variable. If SDF training changes the proportion of low-displacement episodes, dfCAR could create or mask treatment effects. Have you verified that the displacement distribution is comparable across conditions before filtering?\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n- **Intermediate lambda values (0.03, 0.05, 0.07)** to characterize the dose-response curve between the \"no effect\" and \"+15.5pp\" regimes. This is essential for understanding whether SDF is truly redundant or merely under-dosed at lambda<=0.01.\n\n- **Third seed for lambda=0.1 and Phase 2 conditions.** Both currently use only 2 seeds, which is insufficient for any statistical claim. Phase 2 in particular needs at least 3 seeds and ablation over Phase 1 duration.\n\n- **Spatial_bowl LoRA-only control (plan_015).** Without this, the +9pp SDF effect on spatial_bowl cannot be attributed. This was in the original experiment plan but never completed.\n\n- **OpenVLA evaluation.** Even a zero-shot 200-episode evaluation with displacement logging would add a third model family and strengthen the fake safety generalization claim.\n\n- **Real robot validation or sim-to-real discussion.** The dfCAR threshold of 0.1mm is calibrated for MuJoCo where actuator noise is effectively zero. Real sensor noise could require recalibration. At minimum, a discussion of how dfCAR would be adapted to real-world settings is needed.\n\n- **Application of dfCAR to published methods.** Demonstrating that dfCAR changes the conclusions about AEGIS, SafeVLA, or other published safety methods would dramatically strengthen the metric's practical value. Without this, the contribution is speculative.\n\n- **TSR collapse analysis.** Per-task TSR learning curves, comparison with known catastrophic forgetting baselines, and standard mitigation attempts (EWC, replay, LR scheduling) would clarify the mechanism and practical implications.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- Table 1 mixes fine-tuned models (Pi0 A1, LoRA) with zero-shot models (Pi0.5 baseline, OpenVLA projected) without clearly flagging the different experimental conditions. This conflation could mislead readers about architectural vs. training-regime effects.\n\n- The paper does not specify LoRA rank, learning rate, or other training hyperparameters in the main text (they appear only in the method.tex draft as rank 16/32, LR 2.5e-5). These are essential for reproducibility.\n\n- The \"200 episodes per condition (50 per task)\" evaluation protocol is reasonable for detecting large effects but may be underpowered for the fine-grained comparisons attempted (e.g., +0.8pp SDF delta on object_pudding).\n\n- The displacement threshold delta=0.1mm for defining \"fake safe\" is stated but not justified from first principles. While the bimodal gap makes it insensitive in practice, a brief derivation or calibration argument would strengthen the methodology.\n\n- Several tables reference \"seed means\" but do not report whether these are arithmetic means of per-seed CAR or CAR computed on pooled episodes. These can differ when seed-level episode counts vary.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 2/4 — The fake safety diagnosis and oracle experiments are methodologically sound. However, the central SDF attribution claim relies on an underpowered null result (n=3, no equivalence testing), the lambda=0.1 result contradicts the redundancy narrative, and Phase 2 uses only 2 seeds. The circularity between demonstrating EE-only SDF failure (Claim 3) and then testing EE-only SDF redundancy (Claim 4) is a structural flaw.\n- Significance: 2/4 — The fake safety finding is practically relevant for the VLA community. However, dfCAR is technically straightforward, the SDF redundancy claim is both underpowered and confounded, and the narrow scope (1 benchmark, 2 related model families, 1-2 scenes) limits generalizability. The TSR collapse may be the most important finding but is underanalyzed.\n- Clarity: 3/4 — The diagnostic sections are well-written and the narrative arc from fake safety through attribution is logical. Tables are informative. The paper loses clarity in the statistical claims, where p-values are reported without test details, confidence intervals, or degrees of freedom.\n- Overall: 4/10 — The paper identifies a genuine and practically relevant problem (fake safety metric contamination) and proposes a sensible correction (dfCAR). The oracle experiment design is a highlight. However, the headline experimental claim (SDF redundancy) is underpowered, potentially circular, and contradicted by the lambda=0.1 result within the same paper. The experimental scope is too narrow for the generality of the title and claims. Two critical experiments are missing (OpenVLA, spatial_bowl LoRA control). The two-phase training result, while promising, rests on 2 seeds with no hyperparameter exploration. In its current form, the paper is below the CoRL acceptance bar (~40% acceptance rate) due to the combination of insufficient statistical rigor for null claims, narrow scope, and incomplete experimental coverage.\n- Confidence: 4/5 — I am familiar with VLA architectures, SDF-based collision avoidance, SafeLIBERO, and the relevant statistical methodology for evaluating null results in robotics experiments."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aab3c4458efb3fb45 (use SendMessage with to: 'aab3c4458efb3fb45' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 89261\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 152888</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c2499d0-d3bb-4c09-8644-35391fa8435e","uuid":"b4a497af-3f07-4d7a-9a5b-a3f77fef07e4","timestamp":"2026-05-26T03:38:40.852Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer R1** for a robotics/ML conference (CoRL). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue Standards (CoRL)\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=seminal, 8-9=strong accept, 7=accept, 6=marginal accept, 5=marginal reject, 4=reject, 3=clear reject, 1-2=strong reject)\n- Acceptance rate: ~40%\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality (technical soundness), Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning, Limitations\n- Papers must demonstrate relevance to robot learning through intent or outcome\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: Decomposing Collision Avoidance into Implicit Demonstrations and Explicit Geometric Supervision\"\n\nThe paper studies collision avoidance in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models on SafeLIBERO. Key claims:\n\n1. **Fake Safety (Claim 1):** Pi0 model with SDF loss achieves 91.5% CAR but TSR=0% — action collapse, not real avoidance. They propose displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR) that filters out frozen episodes. dfCAR is robust across δ∈[2mm,100mm].\n\n2. **Decomposing Avoidance (Claim 2-3):** LoRA fine-tuning on collision-free demos produces +29pp CAR over baseline (implicit demo signals). SDF auxiliary loss at low λ (≤0.01) adds no significant CAR above LoRA-only. At λ=0.1, SDF adds +15.5pp CAR (p<0.02) — dose-dependent effect.\n\n3. **Two-Phase Training (Claim 4):** SDF pre-training (10K steps) → task-only fine-tuning → CAR=66%, TSR=40% (2-seed mean), partially resolving safety-capability tradeoff.\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n\n**Models:** Pi0 (condition A1), Pi0.5 baseline, LoRA fine-tuned Pi0.5. OpenVLA planned but BLOCKED (model download issues — only projected values reported).\n\n**Evaluation:** 200 episodes per condition (50 per task), object_pudding scene (4 tasks). Metrics: TSR, CAR, EE displacement, frozen rate.\n\n**Tables:**\n- Table 1: Fake safety diagnosis. A1: TSR=0%, CAR=91%, Fake Safe Rate=99.4%, EE Disp (safe eps)=0.005mm. Pi0.5 baseline: TSR=73%, CAR=28%. OpenVLA projected: TSR=0%, CAR=88%.\n- Table 2: dfCAR threshold sensitivity. A1 dfCAR=0.4% at all thresholds. LoRA dfCAR=54.5% constant. SDF λ=0.005 dfCAR=52.3% constant. Baseline dfCAR=83→80% (mild decrease).\n- Table 3: LoRA-only 3 seeds. s0: TSR=8.5%, CAR=54.5%. s1: TSR=4%, CAR=56%. s2: TSR=2%, CAR=44%. Mean CAR=51.5%, SD=6.5pp.\n- Table 4: 3v3 SDF attribution. LoRA-only CAR=51.5% (SE=3.7pp). SDF λ=0.005: 52.3% (SE=1.5pp, Δ=+0.8pp, p>0.5). SDF λ=0.01: 52.8% (SE=0.3pp, Δ=+1.3pp, p>0.3). SDF λ=0.1: 67% (SE=1.0pp, Δ=+15.5pp, p<0.02).\n- Table 5: Lambda dose-response. Flat for λ≤0.01, jumps at λ=0.1.\n- Table 6: Two-phase results. Phase 2 seed 2: CAR=68%, TSR=42%. Phase 2 seed 0: CAR=64%, TSR=38%. Mean: CAR=66%, TSR=40%.\n- Table 7: Cross-scene. object_pudding: LoRA CAR=51.5%, SDF CAR=52.3%, Δ=+0.8pp (ns). spatial_bowl: LoRA CAR=36%, SDF CAR=45%, Δ=+9pp (marginal). NOT consistent across scenes.\n- Table 8: Training convergence. All stable.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 15 experiments. Key gaps relative to the paper:\n- **plan_002 (OpenVLA zero-shot):** BLOCKED. Paper reports only projected values. Only 2 model families tested.\n- **plan_012 (Phase 2 additional seed):** Only 2 seeds for two-phase → low statistical power (SE≈±2pp).\n- **plan_013 (LoRA seed2):** CAR=44%, driving high LoRA variance (SD=6.5pp). Makes low-λ SDF attribution unreliable.\n- **plan_015 (spatial_bowl LoRA control):** Only 1 seed per condition. Cross-scene SDF effect inconsistent.\n- Phase 2 only tested from λ=0.005 10K checkpoint, not explored other Phase 1 durations or λ values.\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental Design Quality:**\n   - Are the ablations well-controlled? Is the 3v3 comparison properly powered?\n   - Is 200 episodes per condition sufficient? Is 50 per task enough?\n   - How does the high LoRA variance (SD=6.5pp) affect the statistical claims?\n   - Is the p<0.02 for λ=0.1 reliable given only 3 seeds per condition?\n\n2. **Statistical Rigor:**\n   - Are the statistical tests appropriate? What test was used for the p-values?\n   - With only 3 seeds, what is the effective degrees of freedom?\n   - The LoRA SE=3.7pp vs SDF SE=1.0pp — is unequal variance handled?\n   - Two-phase has only 2 seeds (SE≈±2pp) — can this support a claim?\n\n3. **Methodological Concerns:**\n   - dfCAR definition: Is excluding episodes with low displacement principled? Does it introduce bias?\n   - SDF loss formulation (Eq. 2): EE-only, cannot prevent arm-body collisions. How limiting is this?\n   - Two-phase protocol: Only one Phase 1 duration (10K) and one λ (0.005) tested. How robust?\n   - The \"fake safety\" phenomenon — is this genuinely novel or well-known (action collapse)?\n\n4. **Missing Experiments / Gaps:**\n   - OpenVLA blocked — weakens generalization claim\n   - No real robot experiments\n   - Single scene (object_pudding) for main results; cross-scene inconsistent\n   - No comparison with other safety methods (e.g., constrained RL, safety filters)\n   - Phase 2 not explored with different Phase 1 durations or λ values\n\n5. **Reproducibility:**\n   - Are training details sufficient? (LoRA rank, learning rate, etc. not specified in paper)\n   - Is the code/benchmark available?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's method and experimental contributions]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 4-8 items, each with specific evidence]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What additional experiments would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, presentation issues, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: [1-4] (1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Clarity: [1-4]\n- Overall: [1-10] with justification\n- Confidence: [1-5] (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n","agentId":"aab3c4458efb3fb45","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the context needed. Let me compose the detailed R1 review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThe paper diagnoses systematic contamination of the Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) metric on SafeLIBERO by action collapse (\"fake safety\"), proposes displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR), and conducts a series of ablations decomposing collision avoidance into implicit demonstration signals vs. explicit SDF geometric supervision. The experimental contribution spans 70+ experiments and 6000+ episodes across Pi0 and Pi0.5 model families. The central experimental finding is that LoRA fine-tuning on collision-free demonstrations alone accounts for the full +29pp CAR improvement, with SDF auxiliary loss contributing 0 additional pp at low lambda and a statistically significant +15.5pp only at lambda=0.1 (p<0.02), though the two-phase training protocol partially resolves the safety-capability tradeoff (CAR=66%, TSR=40%).\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-designed fake safety diagnosis (Claim 1).** The 800-episode A1 evaluation with per-episode displacement analysis provides compelling quantitative evidence. The dual evidence chain (99.6% obstacle displacement < 0.1mm AND TSR=0%) is methodologically sound. The bimodal displacement gap spanning 6 orders of magnitude (sub-micrometer vs. 200+ mm) makes the finding robust to analytic choices.\n\n- **Oracle experiment design is methodologically clean.** Testing three GT-SDF oracle variants (adaptive-alpha, fixed-alpha, feature-space) that all underperform the baseline (73-77% vs. 82% CAR) eliminates prediction-error confounds and cleanly isolates the EE-only structural limitation. This is a proper upper-bound experiment.\n\n- **dfCAR threshold sensitivity analysis is thorough.** Demonstrating invariance across delta in [2mm, 100mm] — a 50x range — is a strong robustness property. The fact that A1 dfCAR collapses to 0.4% at every threshold while LoRA dfCAR remains at 54.5% cleanly separates fake from real safety.\n\n- **The 3v3 seed design for SDF attribution (Table 4) is the correct experimental structure.** Using matched seed counts with separate standard errors and reporting p-values for each lambda condition follows good practice for isolating the SDF contribution from LoRA capacity effects.\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative results and iterative course corrections.** The decision log (D009-D010 terminating runtime refinement, D025 overturning SDF attribution) demonstrates scientific integrity. The paper does not hide inconvenient findings.\n\n- **Large cumulative experimental effort.** 70+ experiments, multiple model families, systematic lambda sweeps, phase-scheduled training — the experimental coverage is ambitious for a diagnostic paper.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The 3v3 comparison is critically underpowered for a null-result claim (Table 4).** With n=3 seeds per condition, the effective degrees of freedom for a Welch t-test is approximately 2-4. The LoRA-only condition has SD=6.5pp (SE=3.7pp), meaning the 95% CI on the LoRA-SDF difference at lambda=0.005 spans roughly [-8pp, +10pp]. The paper cannot distinguish \"SDF adds 0pp\" from \"SDF adds 5pp\" — or even \"SDF subtracts 5pp.\" The absence of a formal equivalence test (TOST) with a pre-specified margin is a significant methodological gap. Stating \"SDF contributes 0pp\" based on p>0.5 commits the classic error of treating absence-of-significance as evidence-of-absence. The LoRA seed2 anomaly (CAR=44% vs. s0=54.5%, s1=56%) is a 10pp outlier that drives the high variance and could flip the conclusion if replaced.\n\n- **Unequal variance between conditions is not addressed (Table 4).** LoRA-only SE=3.7pp vs. SDF lambda=0.005 SE=1.5pp represents a ~6x variance ratio. Standard Welch t-tests handle unequal variance but with n=3, the Satterthwaite approximation for degrees of freedom is unreliable. The paper does not report which statistical test was used, the degrees of freedom, or confidence intervals — only p-values. This is below the standard expected for a paper whose central claim rests on a null result.\n\n- **The lambda=0.1 result (Table 4, +15.5pp, p<0.02) is based on only 2 seeds.** A Welch t-test with 2 observations in one group and 3 in the other has at most 2-3 effective degrees of freedom. The p<0.02 from such a test has extremely fragile coverage properties — a single additional seed could easily push p above 0.05. Moreover, the jump from \"no effect at lambda<=0.01\" to \"+15.5pp at lambda=0.1\" (Table 5) with no intermediate lambda values (e.g., 0.03, 0.05) leaves the dose-response curve severely under-sampled. The paper cannot determine whether there is a smooth transition or a phase change, nor at what lambda the effect becomes meaningful.\n\n- **Two-phase training (Table 6) uses only 2 seeds.** The reported CAR=66% (SE~2pp) and TSR=40% (SE~2pp) cannot support strong claims. The 95% CI on TSR spans approximately [36%, 44%], which includes both \"practically useful\" and \"borderline useless\" territory. Only one Phase 1 duration (10K steps) and one lambda (0.005) were tested — the paper cannot determine whether these are optimal or merely the first configuration attempted. No ablation over Phase 1 duration (5K, 10K, 20K) or Phase 1 lambda (0.01, 0.05, 0.1) is provided.\n\n- **Cross-scene results (Table 7) are inconsistent and single-seeded.** The SDF effect on spatial_bowl (+9pp, marginal) differs qualitatively from object_pudding (+0.8pp, ns). With n=1 seed per condition for spatial_bowl, this discrepancy cannot be interpreted — it could reflect genuine scene-dependent effects or seed-level noise. The missing spatial_bowl LoRA-only control (plan_015) prevents proper attribution. The paper's claim that SDF is redundant is based primarily on one scene.\n\n- **The dfCAR metric, while sensible, introduces a potential selection bias.** By excluding low-displacement episodes, dfCAR conditions on a post-treatment variable (displacement). If the treatment (SDF loss) systematically changes which episodes have low displacement, the filtering can create spurious differences or mask real ones. The paper does not discuss this conditioning-on-a-mediator issue. Additionally, dfCAR is defined using EE displacement, but the A1 fake safety analysis (Table 1) uses obstacle displacement — these are different quantities and the paper does not reconcile them.\n\n- **The SDF loss formulation (Eq. 2 in the method) is EE-only, and the paper itself demonstrates this is structurally deficient (Claim 3, oracle experiments).** Testing SDF redundancy using a known-deficient SDF formulation creates circularity: the paper cannot distinguish \"geometric supervision is fundamentally redundant\" (strong, novel) from \"this particular EE-only formulation is too limited to help\" (weak, expected). The positive lambda=0.1 result actually undermines the redundancy claim — it suggests the SDF signal does work when strong enough, contradicting the \"redundant\" narrative.\n\n- **OpenVLA is blocked.** Table 1 reports only \"projected\" values for OpenVLA (TSR=0%, CAR=88%), but the actual evaluation was never completed due to model download failures. This weakens the generalization claim significantly — the paper tests only two model families (Pi0, Pi0.5) from the same architectural lineage. For an evaluation methodology paper claiming to identify a general problem with \"VLA Safety Evaluation,\" this is a critical scope limitation.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Statistical methodology:** What specific test was used for the p-values in Tables 4-5? Please report the test statistic, degrees of freedom, and 95% confidence intervals for each comparison. Have you considered a formal equivalence test (TOST) with a pre-registered equivalence margin? With n=3 seeds, what is the minimum detectable effect size at 80% power?\n\n2. **Lambda dose-response discontinuity:** The jump from \"no effect\" at lambda=0.01 to \"+15.5pp\" at lambda=0.1 is a 10x lambda increase with no intermediate data points. Have you tested lambda in {0.03, 0.05, 0.07}? Is this a smooth transition or a phase change? The lambda=0.1 result potentially contradicts the SDF-redundancy claim — how do you reconcile \"SDF adds nothing\" with \"10x more SDF adds 15.5pp\"?\n\n3. **Circularity in SDF redundancy claim:** Your oracle experiments (Claim 3) demonstrate that EE-only SDF is structurally deficient. You then test SDF redundancy using only EE-only SDF. How do you distinguish \"geometric supervision is fundamentally redundant for VLA collision avoidance\" from \"this particular EE-only SDF formulation is too broken to help at low doses\"?\n\n4. **TSR collapse mechanism:** Both LoRA-only and LoRA+SDF suffer identical catastrophic TSR collapse (73% to 6%). Is this catastrophic forgetting from fine-tuning on a filtered subset of demonstrations? Have you tried standard mitigations (EWC, replay buffer, lower learning rate, higher LoRA rank)? This is arguably the most practically consequential finding and deserves mechanistic analysis.\n\n5. **dfCAR selection bias:** Excluding low-displacement episodes conditions on a post-treatment variable. If SDF training changes the proportion of low-displacement episodes, dfCAR could create or mask treatment effects. Have you verified that the displacement distribution is comparable across conditions before filtering?\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n- **Intermediate lambda values (0.03, 0.05, 0.07)** to characterize the dose-response curve between the \"no effect\" and \"+15.5pp\" regimes. This is essential for understanding whether SDF is truly redundant or merely under-dosed at lambda<=0.01.\n\n- **Third seed for lambda=0.1 and Phase 2 conditions.** Both currently use only 2 seeds, which is insufficient for any statistical claim. Phase 2 in particular needs at least 3 seeds and ablation over Phase 1 duration.\n\n- **Spatial_bowl LoRA-only control (plan_015).** Without this, the +9pp SDF effect on spatial_bowl cannot be attributed. This was in the original experiment plan but never completed.\n\n- **OpenVLA evaluation.** Even a zero-shot 200-episode evaluation with displacement logging would add a third model family and strengthen the fake safety generalization claim.\n\n- **Real robot validation or sim-to-real discussion.** The dfCAR threshold of 0.1mm is calibrated for MuJoCo where actuator noise is effectively zero. Real sensor noise could require recalibration. At minimum, a discussion of how dfCAR would be adapted to real-world settings is needed.\n\n- **Application of dfCAR to published methods.** Demonstrating that dfCAR changes the conclusions about AEGIS, SafeVLA, or other published safety methods would dramatically strengthen the metric's practical value. Without this, the contribution is speculative.\n\n- **TSR collapse analysis.** Per-task TSR learning curves, comparison with known catastrophic forgetting baselines, and standard mitigation attempts (EWC, replay, LR scheduling) would clarify the mechanism and practical implications.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- Table 1 mixes fine-tuned models (Pi0 A1, LoRA) with zero-shot models (Pi0.5 baseline, OpenVLA projected) without clearly flagging the different experimental conditions. This conflation could mislead readers about architectural vs. training-regime effects.\n\n- The paper does not specify LoRA rank, learning rate, or other training hyperparameters in the main text (they appear only in the method.tex draft as rank 16/32, LR 2.5e-5). These are essential for reproducibility.\n\n- The \"200 episodes per condition (50 per task)\" evaluation protocol is reasonable for detecting large effects but may be underpowered for the fine-grained comparisons attempted (e.g., +0.8pp SDF delta on object_pudding).\n\n- The displacement threshold delta=0.1mm for defining \"fake safe\" is stated but not justified from first principles. While the bimodal gap makes it insensitive in practice, a brief derivation or calibration argument would strengthen the methodology.\n\n- Several tables reference \"seed means\" but do not report whether these are arithmetic means of per-seed CAR or CAR computed on pooled episodes. These can differ when seed-level episode counts vary.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 2/4 — The fake safety diagnosis and oracle experiments are methodologically sound. However, the central SDF attribution claim relies on an underpowered null result (n=3, no equivalence testing), the lambda=0.1 result contradicts the redundancy narrative, and Phase 2 uses only 2 seeds. The circularity between demonstrating EE-only SDF failure (Claim 3) and then testing EE-only SDF redundancy (Claim 4) is a structural flaw.\n- Significance: 2/4 — The fake safety finding is practically relevant for the VLA community. However, dfCAR is technically straightforward, the SDF redundancy claim is both underpowered and confounded, and the narrow scope (1 benchmark, 2 related model families, 1-2 scenes) limits generalizability. The TSR collapse may be the most important finding but is underanalyzed.\n- Clarity: 3/4 — The diagnostic sections are well-written and the narrative arc from fake safety through attribution is logical. Tables are informative. The paper loses clarity in the statistical claims, where p-values are reported without test details, confidence intervals, or degrees of freedom.\n- Overall: 4/10 — The paper identifies a genuine and practically relevant problem (fake safety metric contamination) and proposes a sensible correction (dfCAR). The oracle experiment design is a highlight. However, the headline experimental claim (SDF redundancy) is underpowered, potentially circular, and contradicted by the lambda=0.1 result within the same paper. The experimental scope is too narrow for the generality of the title and claims. Two critical experiments are missing (OpenVLA, spatial_bowl LoRA control). The two-phase training result, while promising, rests on 2 seeds with no hyperparameter exploration. In its current form, the paper is below the CoRL acceptance bar (~40% acceptance rate) due to the combination of insufficient statistical rigor for null claims, narrow scope, and incomplete experimental coverage.\n- Confidence: 4/5 — I am familiar with VLA architectures, SDF-based collision avoidance, SafeLIBERO, and the relevant statistical methodology for evaluating null results in robotics experiments."}],"totalDurationMs":152888,"totalTokens":89261,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":751,"cache_read_input_tokens":84898,"output_tokens":3609,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":751},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3609,"cache_read_input_tokens":84898,"cache_creation_input_tokens":751,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":751,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a142ef3cb1c3fdad8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzJgRpakVmGTQ26MbPjsQK","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","usage":{"total_tokens":85283,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":173045},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"fe29ab68-fb0a-4dc0-ab8f-09087ec6769f","session_id":"9c2499d0-d3bb-4c09-8644-35391fa8435e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BEiuJHn3RFdr2fJs7gm9pw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MZoiLETygPyqfqJ4Ma8T5u","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper identifies that Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) in VLA safety evaluation is systematically contaminated by action collapse (frozen robots registering as \"safe\"), proposes displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR) as a corrective metric, and demonstrates through ablation that collision avoidance in fine-tuned VLAs originates from implicit demonstration signals rather than explicit SDF geometric supervision. The paper has pivoted from a method paper to a diagnostic/negative-finding paper, and its novelty rests primarily on the empirical observation that CAR is unreliable and the decomposition of avoidance sources.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The \"fake safety\" observation is well-documented and impactful.** The 91.5% CAR / 0% TSR / 99.6% frozen-episode finding on A1 is a striking empirical result. Even if action collapse is conceptually unsurprising, demonstrating that it inflates a widely-used safety metric by this magnitude is a genuine contribution to the VLA safety evaluation community. The 800-episode analysis is thorough.\n\n- **dfCAR threshold robustness is convincing.** The bimodal gap (>200mm between frozen and active episodes) that renders dfCAR invariant across delta in [2, 200] mm is a clean result. This eliminates the obvious reviewer objection about threshold sensitivity. The metric is simple, principled, and immediately usable by practitioners.\n\n- **The SDF redundancy finding is well-controlled.** The 4-point lambda dose-response curve (0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1), cross-scene validation (object_pudding + spatial_bowl with opposite-sign deltas), and Phase 2 persistence ablation constitute a thorough negative result. The flat curve with R^2=0.12 across a 20x lambda range is convincing evidence of structural redundancy, not merely poor tuning.\n\n- **Honest and detailed limitation disclosure.** The paper acknowledges restricted scope (2 model families, 2 scenes, OpenVLA blocked), low seed counts, and the distinction between conservative behavior and genuine safety awareness. This intellectual honesty is refreshing and appropriate for a diagnostic paper.\n\n- **Per-task breakdown reveals mechanistic insight.** The consistent per-task CAR pattern across all seeds and conditions (task geometry dominates, not SDF strength) provides mechanistic understanding beyond aggregate numbers.\n\n- **The related_work_v2.tex draft (not compiled into the dream paper) demonstrates awareness of the relevant literature landscape.** The three-way taxonomy (post-hoc correction / constrained optimization / inference-time guidance) for collision-safe VLA methods is well-organized.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Action collapse as a failure mode is not novel; the novelty is in documenting its metric contamination.** Action collapse / mode collapse is extensively documented in RL (degenerate policies), generative modeling (mode collapse in GANs/diffusion), and imitation learning (behavioral cloning averaging over multimodal demonstrations; Chi et al., RSS 2023). The paper's own literature map includes Dong et al. (2025) on \"loss collapse\" in diffusion policies — essentially the same phenomenon. The contribution is therefore narrower than the title \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation\" suggests: it is \"CAR does not account for action collapse,\" which, while useful, is closer to a benchmark/metrics paper than a conceptual advance.\n\n- **The bibliography in the compiled dream paper is severely incomplete and partially fabricated.** The best-tier paper.tex contains 12 references, of which at least 4 are placeholder entries with \"{Authors}\" as the author field (sdf_safety2024, safe_diffusion2025, neural_sdf2023, diff_sdf2024, safety_metrics2023). These are not real papers — they appear to be invented citations. This is a critical deficiency. The project's own literature_map.yaml identifies 25+ real papers that should be cited but are not. A CoRL submission with fabricated bibliography entries would be desk-rejected.\n\n- **dfCAR is conceptually simple — it is displacement thresholding + CAR.** While simplicity is a virtue, the intellectual contribution of \"filter out frozen episodes before computing a rate\" is modest. The bimodal gap that makes it threshold-robust is a property of this specific benchmark/model combination, not a guaranteed property of all VLA evaluations — the paper does not discuss what happens when the gap is not bimodal (partial collapse, slow-moving policies).\n\n- **The SDF redundancy negative finding has limited scope for generalization.** The SDF loss is EE-only, applied to a specific VLA family (Pi0.5), on a specific benchmark (SafeLIBERO), with LoRA fine-tuning. The paper's own analysis identifies the structural limitation (EE-only SDF misses arm-body collisions), meaning the negative finding is partially expected from first principles. A full-body SDF or C-space distance field (Li et al., RSS 2024) might yield different results. The paper frames SDF redundancy as a general conclusion (\"implicit demonstration signals, not explicit geometric supervision, drive collision avoidance\"), which overgeneralizes from EE-only SDF to all geometric supervision.\n\n- **The \"decomposition\" into implicit (demo) vs. explicit (SDF) is a two-condition ablation, not a framework.** Calling it a \"decomposition\" or \"framework\" overstates the methodological contribution. A genuine decomposition framework would need to disentangle multiple sources (data distribution, model capacity, training procedure, geometric signal) more systematically.\n\n- **Only 2 model families tested, both from the Pi0 lineage.** The blocked OpenVLA experiment is a significant gap for a paper claiming to \"rethink VLA safety evaluation\" — the claim of generality is currently unsupported beyond the Pi0 family.\n\n- **The TSR collapse (73% to 6%) under LoRA fine-tuning is not adequately analyzed.** The paper does not distinguish between the model learning to be conservative (safety-relevant) and the model losing task capability due to distribution shift (a fine-tuning artifact). Without this distinction, the claim that \"fine-tuning produces genuine collision avoidance\" is weakened.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On dfCAR generality:** The bimodal gap arises because action collapse in Pi0 produces near-zero displacement. What happens with models that exhibit partial collapse (slow but not frozen movement)? Is dfCAR robust in that regime?\n\n2. **On the negative finding scope:** You conclude that \"implicit demonstration signals, not explicit geometric supervision, drive collision avoidance.\" But your geometric supervision is EE-only SDF, which you identify as structurally limited. Would you expect the same conclusion for full-body SDF or C-space distance fields?\n\n3. **On the TSR collapse mechanism:** Can you provide evidence that the 73% to 6% TSR drop reflects learned conservatism rather than catastrophic forgetting? Do fine-tuned models approach obstacles and retreat, or fail to reach task regions entirely?\n\n4. **On cross-model generalization:** Your title claims \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation,\" but all results are on the Pi0 family. Can you provide any evidence that fake safety is not Pi0-specific?\n\n5. **On CAR-TSR anti-correlation:** The observation that models attempting more tasks collide more is near-tautological. What would constitute a surprising violation of this pattern?\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n- **Safe RL foundations:** CPO (Achiam et al., ICML 2017), Lagrangian methods for constrained MDPs. SafeVLA (Zhang et al., NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight) gets only one sentence.\n- **Action collapse / mode collapse:** Diffusion Policy (Chi et al., RSS 2023), Dong et al. (2025) on loss collapse in diffusion policies.\n- **Other geometric representations:** C-space distance fields (Li et al., RSS 2024), full-body SDF (Zhu et al., 2024), Neural NMPC with SDF (Jacquet et al., IJRR 2025).\n- **Metric validity in ML:** Goodhart's law literature, benchmark saturation (Gururangan et al., 2018).\n- **VLA foundations:** RT-2, OpenVLA, Octo, LIBERO benchmark proper citation.\n- **Runtime safety methods:** RAIL, TamedPUMA, APEX — all in the project's literature map but absent from the paper.\n- **Spatial/3D-aware VLAs:** Spatial Forcing (ICLR 2026), QDepth-VLA — relevant as geometric auxiliary losses similar in spirit.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- Title mismatch across drafts; should settle on one.\n- Fabricated bibliography entries ({Authors}) must be replaced with real citations.\n- Welch t-test with df~3 has extremely low power; \"SDF contributes 0 pp\" should be hedged as \"no detectable effect at our sample size.\"\n- 8 tables in 7 pages is dense; some could be consolidated or moved to appendix.\n- Discussion section is thin — missing comparison to concurrent work, implications for VLA architecture design, connection to safe RL theory.\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: 2 (fair) — Action collapse is known; dfCAR is simple thresholding; SDF redundancy partially expected from EE-only limitation. Useful empirical combination but no single highly novel component.\n- Significance: 2 (fair) — Metric contamination finding is practically useful but scope is narrow (1 benchmark, 2 model families, 2 scenes) for the breadth of claims.\n- Clarity: 3 (good) — Well-written, logically structured, appropriately hedged. Main issue is fabricated/placeholder references.\n- Overall: 5 (marginal reject) — Valid empirical contribution but incremental novelty, narrow scope for the claims, critically incomplete bibliography with fabricated entries, and \"framework\" framing overstates a two-condition ablation. Below CoRL acceptance bar in current form. Fixable with proper references, scoped claims, and additional model families.\n- Confidence: 4 (high) — Familiar with VLA safety, SDF-in-robotics, and safe RL literatures.","project":"diff_collision_sdf","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260526T023936","overall_score":"marginal reject (5/10)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":742,"cache_read_input_tokens":84278,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":742,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzJgRpakVmGTQ26MbPjsQK","session_id":"9c2499d0-d3bb-4c09-8644-35391fa8435e","uuid":"ace199d1-dae6-47f5-813b-eea2d75eba4e"}
